@cfriedt @niflostancu don't want to hijack this PR but I was working on the same driver few weeks ago, there are still some TODOs (see the commit message for the driver code) but maybe you could make use of it or part of it? I pushed my version here: https://github.com/pepe2k/zephyr/tree/ti-cc13xx-cc26xx_watchdog-driver

Cheers,
Piotr

Hi, thank you for your suggestions.
Based on them, I've done some extensive modifications to the PR:

- rewritten init code to use `DT_INST_FOREACH_STATUS_OKAY`;
- implemented NMI mode (using a new boolean DTSI property: `interrupt-nmi`; hope that's the best way to do it);
- replaced msToTicks conversion function with a macro;
- move the `_enable()` code inside `_setup()`;
- if WDT is enabled at boot, call `_setup()` on init with the default option `WDT_OPT_PAUSE_HALTED_BY_DBG` based on `CONFIG_DEBUG`;
- enabled WDT feature for all supported boards (in separate commit, incl. doc updates);
- better comments explaining the implementation decisions made.

Note: as various TI forum posts suggest [1] (I couldn't find any explicit mentions in the Reference), the watchdog clock is always paused during sleep, so there is  nothing to be done in this case (I avoided any option checks for this to be permissive).

Since I based my solution on some of @pepe2k's code, how shall we proceed with the copyright notice in the header? Shall I insert his name + email too, separated by a comma?

Thanks.

[1] https://e2e.ti.com/support/wireless-connectivity/sub-1-ghz-group/sub-1-ghz/f/sub-1-ghz-forum/990138/cc1312r-hw-watchdog-strange-behavior-in-debug/3666163#3666163
